When two or more nouns are joined by "and" and each noun possesses something separately (not jointly), each noun needs its own possessive apostrophe ('s).

The Rule

Separate possession → each noun gets its own 's
Joint/shared possession → only the last noun gets 's

Examples

(a) I went to Ram's and Sita's house and found the couple missing.

✅ Ram and Sita's house (one shared house — matches "the couple" living together)

(b) Mala's and Priya's account.

Two separate accounts — one belonging to Mala, one belonging to Priya.
Both get 's: Mala's and Priya's.

(c) Mala's and Priya's joint account.

Wait — this looks like it should be joint possession (one shared account), so this is actually testing the contrast.
✅ Correct form for joint possession: Mala and Priya's joint account (only the last name gets 's, since it's one shared account).
❌ "Mala's and Priya's joint account" — technically inconsistent, since giving both names 's implies separate ownership, which contradicts "joint."
